message.content:
{
  "topic_id": "eukaryotic_mitosis",
  "audience": "high_school",
  "facts": [
    {
      "fact_id": "F1",
      "claim": "During S phase of interphase, DNA replication produces two identical sister chromatids per chromosome, firmly attached at the centromeric region.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; S phase of interphase",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["E_70c6108994", "E_d79c940d49"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_70c6108994",
          "quote": "In the S phase , DNA replication can proceed through the mechanisms that result in the formation of identical pairs of DNA molecules—sister chromatids—that are firmly attached at the centromeric region."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "In the S phase (synthesis phase), DNA replication results in the formation of two identical copies of each chromosome—sister chromatids—that are firmly attached at the centromere region."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact_id": "F2",
      "claim": "Centrosome duplication begins during S phase and is completed in G2; the two centrosomes give rise to the mitotic spindle.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; animal cells with centrosomes; S and G2 phases",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["E_70c6108994", "E_d79c940d49"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_70c6108994",
          "quote": "The centrosome is also duplicated during the S phase. The two centrosomes will give rise to the mitotic spindle"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "The duplication of the centrosomes is completed."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact_id": "F3",
      "claim": "The mitotic phase consists of nuclear division (mitosis/karyokinesis), proceeding through prophase, prometaphase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase, followed by cytokinesis.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; mitotic phase",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["E_70c6108994", "E_d79c940d49"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_70c6108994",
          "quote": "Karyokinesis, also known as mitosis , is divided into a series of phases—prophase, prometaphase, metaphase, anaphase, and telophase—that result in the division of the cell nucleus"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "The first portion of the mitotic phase, mitosis , is composed of five stages, which accomplish nuclear division. The second portion of the mitotic phase, called cytokinesis, is the physical separation of the cytoplasmic components into two daughter cells."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact_id": "F4",
      "claim": "In prometaphase, the nuclear envelope fragments further, chromosomes condense into discrete structures, and kinetochores at centromeres bind spindle microtubules; sister chromatid kinetochores face opposite poles.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; prometaphase",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["E_70c6108994"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_70c6108994",
          "quote": "The remnants of the nuclear envelope fragment further, and the mitotic spindle continues to develop as more microtubules assemble and stretch across the length of the former nuclear area."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_70c6108994",
          "quote": "Each sister chromatid develops a protein structure called a kinetochore in its centromeric region"
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_70c6108994",
          "quote": "the chromosome will be oriented until the kinetochores of sister chromatids face the opposite poles"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact_id": "F5",
      "claim": "The spindle checkpoint near the end of metaphase ensures all sister chromatids are attached to spindle microtubules from opposite poles before anaphase separation proceeds.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; metaphase-to-anaphase transition",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["E_d79c940d49"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "The M checkpoint occurs near the end of the metaphase stage of mitosis. The M checkpoint is also known as the spindle checkpoint because it determines if all the sister chromatids are correctly attached to the spindle microtubules."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "the cycle will not proceed until the kinetochores of each pair of sister chromatids are firmly anchored to spindle fibers arising from opposite poles of the cell."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact_id": "F6",
      "claim": "In telophase, chromosomes reach opposite poles and decondense, mitotic spindles break down, and nuclear envelopes reform around chromosomes.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; telophase",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["E_d79c940d49"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "During telophase , all of the events that set up the duplicated chromosomes for mitosis during the first three phases are reversed. The chromosomes reach the opposite poles and begin to decondense (unravel)."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "The mitotic spindles are broken down into monomers that will be used to assemble cytoskeleton components for each daughter cell. Nuclear envelopes form around chromosomes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact_id": "F7",
      "claim": "Cytokinesis physically separates cytoplasmic components into two daughter cells and can be viewed as a separate process; mitosis can occur without cytokinesis, producing multinucleate cells.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; cytokinesis; exceptions noted",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["E_70c6108994", "E_d79c940d49"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_70c6108994",
          "quote": "interphase and mitosis (karyokinesis) may take place without cytokinesis, in which case cells with multiple nuclei (multinucleate cells) are produced."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "E_d79c940d49",
          "quote": "Cytokinesis is the second part of the mitotic phase during which cell division is completed by the physical separation of the cytoplasmic components into two daughter cells."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact_id": "F8",
      "claim": "The mitotic spindle is a bipolar microtubule-based machine with associated proteins and motor enzymes that organizes duplicated chromosomes and separates them into two sets.",
      "scope": "Eukaryotic cells; mitotic spindle",
      "classification": "established",
      "evidence_ids": ["PMID_27587616"],
      "support": [
        {
          "evidence_id": "PMID_27587616",
          "quote": "All eukaryotic cells prepare for cell division by forming a \"mitotic spindle\"-a bipolar machine made from microtubules (MTs) and many associated proteins."
        },
        {
          "evidence_id": "PMID_27587616",
          "quote": "Both formation and function of the spindle require controlled MT dynamics, as well as the actions of multiple motor enzymes. Spindle-driven motions separate the duplicated chromosomes into two distinct sets"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "mechanism": {
    "events": [
      {
        "event_id": "M1",
        "description": "S phase: DNA replication creates sister chromatids attached at centromeres; centrosome duplication begins.",
        "fact_ids": ["F1", "F2"],
        "roles": ["DNA replication machinery", "sister chromatids", "centromere", "centrosome"],
        "constraints": ["Replication occurs during interphase, not during mitosis"]
      },
      {
        "event_id": "M2",
        "description": "G2: centrosome duplication completes; energy and proteins for chromosome manipulation are synthesized.",
        "fact_ids": ["F2"],
        "roles": ["centrosomes", "energy stores", "cytoskeleton"],
        "constraints": ["Final preparations must complete before mitosis entry"]
      },
      {
        "event_id": "M3",
        "description": "Prometaphase: nuclear envelope fragments; kinetochores bind spindle microtubules; sister kinetochores orient to opposite poles.",
        "fact_ids": ["F4", "F8"],
        "roles": ["nuclear envelope", "kinetochore", "mitotic spindle", "microtubules"],
        "constraints": ["Kinetochores of sister chromatids must face opposite poles"]
      },
      {
        "event_id": "M4",
        "description": "Metaphase checkpoint: spindle checkpoint monitors attachment of all sister chromatids to microtubules from opposite poles before anaphase.",
        "fact_ids": ["F5"],
        "roles": ["spindle checkpoint", "kinetochores", "spindle fibers"],
        "constraints": ["Anaphase separation is irreversible; cycle does not proceed until attachments are correct"]
      },
      {
        "event_id": "M5",
        "description": "Anaphase: spindle-driven motions separate duplicated chromosomes into two distinct sets moved toward opposite ends of the cell.",
        "fact_ids": ["F8", "F3"],
        "roles": ["mitotic spindle", "motor enzymes", "chromosomes"],
        "constraints": ["Requires controlled microtubule dynamics and motor enzyme activity"]
      },
      {
        "event_id": "M6",
        "description": "Telophase: chromosomes decondense, spindles break down, nuclear envelopes reform; nuclear division completes.",
        "fact_ids": ["F6", "F3"],
        "roles": ["chromosomes", "mitotic spindle", "nuclear envelope"],
        "constraints": ["Events of early mitosis are reversed"]
      },
      {
        "event_id": "M7",
        "description": "Cytokinesis: physical separation of cytoplasmic components into two daughter cells; may be absent, yielding multinucleate cells.",
        "fact_ids": ["F7"],
        "roles": ["cytoplasmic components", "daughter cells"],
        "constraints": ["Cytokinesis is distinct from nuclear division and can occur separately"]
      }
    ]
  },
  "assumptions": [
    "The retrieved OpenStax passages describe a generalized eukaryotic cell cycle; specific timing and regulation may differ among organisms.",
    "Centriole presence in centrosomes is not universal (absent in plants and most fungi), so spindle organization details may differ.",
    "Prometaphase is included as a mitotic stage in the sources, though some curricula fold it into prophase.",
    "The molecular details of DNA synthesis directionality (5' to 3' addition at free 3' ends, antiparallel templates, priming/maturation) are not supported by the retrieved passages and are therefore excluded.",
    "Distinguishing sister chromatids from homologous chromosomes in metaphase is not explicitly addressed in the retrieved evidence; the sources state sister chromatids attach to opposite poles but do not detail homologous chromosome behavior.",
    "Evidence gaps: the retrieved passages do not describe prophase events in detail (e.g., chromosome condensation onset, spindle formation initiation) beyond prometaphase continuation; no independent second source verifies telophase or checkpoint details beyond OpenStax.",
    "PMID_27587616 and PMID_32501481 are research abstracts; PMID_32501481 (spindle scaling) was not used for claims because its content concerns scaling mechanisms rather than core mitosis events for a high-school audience."
  ]
}
